intelligence forces have got it right  but for actually to happen i just don't  think we ever we just couldn't believe  that this could happen in our in our  lifetime and it has happened  uh and we can see  uh the consequences of what putin has  chosen to do and as the ft says it's the  largest military advance  since the second world war and it goes  on to describe the scale of the advance  and obviously you've been reporting all  day and all through this evening on how  putin's forces have gathered effectively  around the ukraine  and invaded it in so many different ways  and with devastating consequences and i  obviously heard what deborah haynes had  to say  uh and her words were chilling and i  think that's how we are all today  chilled horrified disgusted  and i think firm in our resolve that we  must now take serious action against  putin  and make it very clear we won't tolerate  this yeah and we'll talk about those  sanctions uh uh in a moment uh john the  front of the guardian uh shows a picture  of a poor woman who's injured in the  street in east ukraine after one of  those air strikes her picture on the  front of a number of papers actually i  mean do you think we underestimated  putin was intending on a full-on  invasion all  along i think the last few weeks putin's  clearly been playing a game with the  west he's had all these leaders and  foreign secretaries etc travelling  to moscow to come and speak to him we've  seen all those pictures of him meeting  leaders at the other far ends of the  table but it looks like whatever the  west had done that this was his plan  all along and as anna said even though  we've been warned that this was what he  was planning to do that he was going to  launch a full-scale invasion is very  much talk different talking about it and  then actually waking up to the news and  seeing the the sheer scale of this you  know with the air land and sea attacks  that he has carried out and obviously  these striking pictures that 